{% block bodyContent %}
<p>Inclusive research involves people with a wide range of needs, backgrounds and circumstances. It helps teams understand barriers, avoid designing around assumptions and create services that work for people who may otherwise be excluded or experience poorer outcomes.</p>

<h2 id="plan-for-inclusion">Plan for inclusion</h2>
<p>Consider who may be affected by the service and who may face barriers because of factors such as:</p>
<ul>
  <li>poverty</li>
  <li>discrimination</li>
  <li>digital exclusion</li>
  <li>language or literacy</li>
  <li>disability</li>
  <li>health</li>
  <li>caring responsibilities</li>
  <li>where they live</li>
</ul>
<p>People's circumstances overlap, so use existing evidence to identify gaps and decide whose experiences you need to understand directly.</p>
<p><a href="/standards-and-technology/service-standard-points/5-make-sure-everyone-can-use-the-service">See NHS service standard 5: Make sure everyone can use the service</a></p>

<h2 id="reach-people-who-are-often-excluded">Reach people who are often excluded</h2>
<p>Do not rely only on people who are easy to reach, confident taking part or already using the service successfully. Work with trusted community organisations where appropriate, allow time to build relationships and recognise their expertise. Follow the <a href="/user-research/recruitment">recruitment guidance in this section</a> to plan a purposeful range of participants.</p>

<h2 id="remove-barriers-to-taking-part">Remove barriers to taking part</h2>
<p>Ask people what they need and offer flexible, accessible ways to take part. Consider the factors in <a href="#plan-for-inclusion">planning for inclusion</a> as well as:</p>
<ul>
  <li>technology</li>
  <li>travel</li>
  <li>timing</li>
  <li>fatigue</li>
  <li>sensory needs</li>
  <li>whether someone needs an interpreter, advocate or support person</li>
</ul>
<p>Do not assume remote research is more accessible for everyone.</p>
<p><a href="https://www.england.nhs.uk/long-read/inclusive-digital-healthcare-a-framework-for-nhs-action-on-digital-inclusion/">Read Inclusive digital healthcare: a framework for NHS action on digital inclusion (NHS England)</a></p>

<h2 id="run-inclusive-research-responsibly">Run inclusive research responsibly</h2>
<p>Make sure you:</p>
<ul>
  <li>use accessible information</li>
  <li>gain informed consent</li>
  <li>adapt the way you facilitate the session</li>
  <li>give people time and breaks</li>
  <li>let them use the tools or support they normally rely on</li>
  <li>plan for sensitive topics, distress and safeguarding concerns</li>
  <li>protect people's identity when sharing findings</li>
</ul>
<p><a href="https://www.gov.uk/service-manual/user-research/running-research-sessions-with-people-with-disabilities">Read about running research sessions with people with disabilities (GOV.UK service manual)</a></p>

<h2 id="recognise-peoples-contribution">Recognise people's contribution</h2>
<p>Offer fair incentives and reimburse reasonable expenses where possible. Tell participants and community partners what happened as a result of their involvement, keep commitments and share findings in an accessible format.</p>
<p>Read <a href="https://www.nihr.ac.uk/support-and-services/industry/inclusive-research">Is your research inclusive? (National Institute for Health and Care Research)</a></p>

